MediaGallery 1.8.0 upgrade notes
===============================

MediaGallery 1.8.0 is intended to support upgrades from the 1.7.x line while
preserving the historical single-site layout by default.

Before upgrading
----------------

1. Back up the Geeklog database.
2. Back up the complete MediaGallery media tree.
3. Record any local MediaGallery modifications.
4. Test the upgrade on a staging or local copy before production.
5. Confirm the target PHP/Geeklog combination is supported by your test build.

Standard single-site installations
----------------------------------

No new siteconfig.php entry is required.

If $_CONF['images_url'] is not defined, MediaGallery keeps the historical media
location:

    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/

and the historical public URL:

    {site_url}/mediagallery/mediaobjects

MediaGallery 1.8.0 does not automatically move existing media files.

Multisite / site-specific image storage
---------------------------------------

Shared-code multisite installations can opt in to site-specific MediaGallery
storage by defining both a physical image path and its matching public URL in
the site's siteconfig.php:

    $_CONF['path_images'] = $_CONF['path'] . 'public_html/images/SITE/';
    $_CONF['images_url']  = $_CONF['site_url'] . '/images/SITE';

When both values are present, MediaGallery 1.8.0 derives:

    $_MG_CONF['path_mediaobjects']
        = rtrim($_CONF['path_images'], '/\\') . '/mediagallery/';

    $_MG_CONF['mediaobjects_url']
        = rtrim($_CONF['images_url'], '/') . '/mediagallery';

If $_CONF['path_data'] is also site-specific, MediaGallery derives private
working directories below it for temporary/upload staging files.

Important: adding $_CONF['images_url'] prepares a site for the 1.8.0 behavior
but does not move media. Existing installations must ensure their current media
files already exist at the path that 1.8.0 will resolve, or copy them manually
during a controlled migration.

Configuration migration
-----------------------

The 1.8.0 upgrade adds missing MediaGallery Configuration API values without
overwriting existing administrator values. Zero/false values are preserved.

The historical manually edited MediaGallery config.php is not restored.
Derived paths and URLs remain runtime values rather than arbitrary
administrator settings.

Security changes to test
------------------------

The 1.8.0 development line includes stricter upload handling:

* browser uploads use Geeklog CSRF protection;
* executable/server-side filename extensions are rejected in user upload flows;
* remote image fetching rejects localhost/private/reserved targets;
* remote-media forms use Geeklog CSRF protection;
* legacy asynchronous uploads no longer trust a POSTed user ID.

FTP/batch import validation is still part of the active 1.8.0 hardening work
and must be tested before a release candidate is considered production-ready.

Recommended test sequence
-------------------------

1. Upgrade a copy of MediaGallery 1.7.0.
2. Upgrade a copy of MediaGallery 1.7.3.
3. Perform a fresh 1.8.0 installation.
4. Test on Geeklog 2.1.1 / PHP 5.6 when legacy compatibility is required.
5. Test on Geeklog 2.2.2 / PHP 8.1 or 8.3.
6. Verify album display, thumbnails, originals, media editing and deletion.
7. Verify browser upload, moderation email and upload rejection cases.
8. Verify Remote Media with normal public URLs and rejected local/private URLs.
9. Verify multisite paths independently for every configured site.
10. Verify a standard site with no $_CONF['images_url'] still uses the legacy
    media path unchanged.

Test archives
-------------

Development test packages are generated manually from GitHub Actions using the
workflow:

    Build MediaGallery installable archive

The workflow creates an installable ZIP with a single mediagallery/ top-level
directory plus a SHA-256 checksum. Generated archives are workflow artifacts
and are not committed to the repository.

Production warning
------------------

The modernize-1.8.0 branch is a development branch. Do not deploy it to a
production site without a full backup and successful staging tests.
